A||N||2||DaveL||Low Pressure Question||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||06-13-2001||02:26 PM|||| I recently had a new compressor, dryer, orifice installed in my 87 SE 2.5, changed over to the r-134. It isn't quite as cold now as it was when I first did it, about 3 weeks ago. How much pressure should I be reading at the low pressure port on the dryer? Also, I've read the coolant should be  put in as gas instead of liquid. Why?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||06-13-2001||03:37 PM||||25 to 30 on the low side should be about right.<P>When I switched, I used about a 27 ounce charge and it's plenty cold.<P>Remember, original Fiero A/C hoses are not barrier material, so you may lose charge over time. There may be another leak that could be losing charge as well.<P>Hope you changed out all the O rings as the old ones may not be up to R134 exposure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||06-13-2001||10:42 PM||||Compressors are made to pump gas,not liquid.If you charge a system with gas,it will take forever.You can add refrigerant in the liquid state carefully,not too fast.When you see it start to frost at the suction valve,stop letting in refrigerant till it clears,then add again.||||reg||1
